1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
A two-level cache answers every access in exactly one of three ways.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Case one is an L1 hit: probability 0.90, cost 1 cycle, and nothing below is touched.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Case two is an L1 miss and an L2 hit: 0.10 times 0.75 is 0.075 of all accesses, costing 1 plus 8 cycles.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Case three is a miss in both: 0.10 times 0.25 is 0.025, costing 1 plus 8 plus 100 cycles.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
The average is the weighted sum of the three costs: 4.3 cycles per access.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Regrouping the same sum gives T1 plus m1 T2 plus m1 m2 T3, which is still 4.3.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
The factored form is an identity, not a shortcut: it is the same sum regrouped.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
The common wrong form uses m2 alone in the last term: 26.8 cycles instead of 4.3.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
A question may print each level's own access time, or a time that already includes the levels above.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
GATE CSE 2025 Set 1 prints 10, 20 and 200 nanoseconds, each already including the miss penalty of the levels above.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
Weighting those three path costs gives 11.85 nanoseconds, which is inside the official key.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Adding 20 and 200 as if they were extra penalties gives 12.5, the value the key rejects.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
A local miss rate divides by the accesses that reached that level; a global one divides by all L1 accesses.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
GATE CSE 2017 Set 1: 1.4 memory accesses per instruction, so 1,400 accesses per 1,000 instructions.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
L1 misses 10 per cent of 1,400, which is 140; L2 misses 7 of those 140, so its local miss rate is 0.05.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
Its global miss rate is 0.05 times 0.10, which is 0.005: one access in 200.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
When the average is given and the miss rate is not, the identity becomes an equation to solve.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
GATE CSE 2017 Set 2: L1 access 1 cycle, L2 access 8, main memory penalty 18, and L1 misses twice as often as L2.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
That is 9x squared plus 8x minus 1 equals 0, whose roots are one ninth and minus one.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
A rate cannot be negative, so the L1 miss rate is 0.111 and the L2 miss rate is 0.056.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
Hierarchical access means the levels are tried in turn, so the times add along the path.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
GATE CSE 2016: 1 plus 0.2 times 10 plus 0.2 times 0.1 times 500 is 13.0 nanoseconds.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
Simultaneous access searches the levels together, so a hit in L1 costs only the L1 time.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
So the same data gives 13.0 or 12.8 depending on one sentence in the question.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
A processor is measured in cycles per instruction, so a per-access cost has to be converted.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Stall cycles per instruction is memory accesses per instruction times the extra cost of one access.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
With 1.4 accesses per instruction, the stalls are 1.4 times 3.3, which is 4.62 cycles.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Added to a base CPI of 1.0, the CPI is 5.62: one cycle of work became five and a half.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
Halving the L1 miss rate gives an average of 2.65 cycles and a CPI of 3.31, a speedup of 1.70.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Halving the L2 local miss rate instead gives 3.05 cycles and a CPI of 3.87, a speedup of 1.45.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
The inner level wins because every L1 miss multiplies the cost of everything below it.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
Neither change halves the time, because the base CPI stays; that is Amdahl's argument in one line.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
Trap one: using m2 where the product m1 m2 belongs gives 26.8 instead of 4.3.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
Trap two: adding a time that already includes the level above gives 12.5 instead of 11.85.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Trap three: quoting a global miss rate as the local one changes the answer by the factor m1.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
Trap four: treating the average access time as a per-instruction cost, which is wrong by the accesses-per-instruction factor.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Every one of these questions is the same partition, with different numbers printed.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
First read which time is given: a level's own time, or a time that already contains the levels above.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Second read the denominator of every rate, and whether the unit is cycles, nanoseconds, or per instruction.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Then write the three outcomes, weight them, and only afterwards look at the options.

